# Minimal Implementation Plan

## Dependencies
- **Odyc.js**: Core game framework
- **TypeScript**: Compile to vanilla JavaScript
- **NES.css**: Retro styling framework (https://nostalgic-css.github.io/NES.css/)

## Emoji Assets
- **Wall**: 🚧 (construction barrier)
- **Labour (playing)**: 👨🏻‍🔧 (mechanic before helmet)
- **Labour (won)**: 👷🏻‍♂️ (construction worker with helmet)
- **Helmet (goal)**: 🪖 (military helmet at fixed bottom right)

## File Structure
```
/src
  index.html          # Game container with NES.css styling
  game.ts            # Single TypeScript file with all game logic
```

## Implementation Steps

### 1. HTML Structure (index.html) - User Updated
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>No Helmet Game</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/nes.css@latest/css/nes.min.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Press+Start+2P" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    body { font-family: "Press Start 2P"; background: #212529; }
    .container { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px; }
    #game-canvas { border: 4px solid #fff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="nes-container with-title is-centered">
      <p class="title">No Helmet Game</p>
      <div id="timer" class="nes-text is-warning">Time: 30s</div>
      <canvas id="game-canvas"></canvas>
      <div id="ui">
        <p id="status" class="nes-text is-primary">Press WASD to start! Find the helmet in 30 seconds!</p>
        <button id="retry-btn" class="nes-btn is-success" style="display:none;">Try Again</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <script src="game.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 2. NES.css Styling Integration
- **Retro Theme**: 8-bit style containers and buttons
- **Pixel Font**: "Press Start 2P" for authentic retro feel  
- **NES Components**: Use nes-container, nes-btn, nes-text classes
- **Color Scheme**: Dark background with retro accent colors
- **Responsive**: Container-based layout for different screen sizes

### 3. Enhanced Maze Generation with Wall Constraint

**Helmet Wall Constraint**
- Helmet position at (size-2, size-2) must never have walls
- Maze generation ensures helmet cell is always open path
- Wall placement algorithm avoids helmet coordinates
- Validation step confirms no walls at helmet position

**Enhanced Maze Class**
- Single-width path network generation (1x1 corridors only)
- Extensive fake path addition (25-35 misleading branches)
- Fixed helmet placement at bottom right (size-2, size-2)
- Wall constraint: no walls at helmet position
- BFS complexity analysis for min/max path distances
- Path width enforcement to prevent wide corridors

### 4. BFS Complexity Evaluation System

**BFS Complexity Analysis**
- Calculate shortest path distance from start to helmet
- Calculate longest possible path distance in maze
- Evaluate difference between min/max distances
- Ensure complexity threshold is met for optimal difficulty

**1x1 Path Width Control**
- Enforce single-cell width for all walkable paths
- Prevent wide corridors that make navigation too easy
- Ensure precise navigation through narrow passages
- Validate path width during generation

### 5. Single File Architecture (TypeScript) - Advanced Features

**All-in-One game.ts Structure**
- 30-second countdown
- Start on first WASD press
- Update display every second
- Trigger game over when expired

**Labour Class**
- Position tracking
- Emoji rendering (👨🏻‍🔧 / 👷🏻‍♂️)
- Movement validation
- Fixed helmet touch detection at bottom right

**Maze Class**
- Complex grid generation with BFS evaluation
- Fixed helmet placement at bottom right corner
- Wall constraint: ensure no walls at helmet position
- Single-width path enforcement (1x1 corridors)
- BFS complexity analysis and validation
- Emoji-based rendering (🚧 for walls, 🪖 for helmet)

**Game Class**
- Odyc.js integration
- Game state management (waiting/playing/won/lost)
- Timer integration
- Retry functionality with new BFS-evaluated maze

### 6. Advanced Features Integration
- **Millisecond Precision Timer**: Track completion time with 100ms accuracy
- **Performance Analytics**: Calculate path efficiency, optimal moves, and scoring
- **Achievement System**: Unlock challenges based on performance metrics
- **Adaptive Difficulty**: Adjust maze complexity based on success rate
- **Dynamic Elements**: Trap cells, checkpoints, and optional moving walls
- **Heat Map Generation**: Visual analysis of attempted paths for learning
- **Progressive Scoring**: Bonus points for speed and optimal route discovery
- **Leaderboard System**: Track personal bests and top completion times

### 7. Enhanced Maze Generation Algorithm with Advanced Complexity
```typescript
1. Use Recursive Backtracking with proper complexity (Wikipedia algorithm)
2. Implement Hunt-and-Kill algorithm for increased path complexity
3. Add Wilson's algorithm for uniform spanning tree complexity
4. Ensure helmet position (size-2, size-2) never has walls
5. Create 35-50 fake dead-end paths with BFS complexity evaluation (increased from 25-35)
6. Each fake path must have closed BFS routes to mislead players
7. Avoid simple "L" shaped direct paths - use complex winding routes
8. Validate at least one correct route exists to helmet (but complex, not direct)
9. Use multi-tier BFS to measure path complexity and ensure optimal difficulty
10. Maintain strict single-width (1x1) path enforcement throughout
11. Add trap cells that temporarily slow movement for 2 seconds
12. Place hidden checkpoints for progress saving in complex sections
13. Implement optional moving walls that shift every 10 seconds
14. Optimize generation to complete within 500ms for smooth gameplay
```

**Advanced Algorithm Requirements**
- **Algorithm Fusion**: Combine multiple Wikipedia algorithms for maximum complexity
- **Dynamic Complexity**: Adjust difficulty based on player performance history
- **Trap Integration**: Strategically place trap cells to increase navigation challenge
- **Checkpoint System**: Hidden progress save points in longer maze sections
- **Moving Elements**: Optional dynamic walls for advanced challenge modes
- **Performance Optimization**: Sub-500ms generation time requirement

### 8. Advanced Performance Analytics System
- **Precision Timing**: Millisecond accuracy for competitive scoring
- **Path Efficiency**: Calculate optimal vs actual path ratio
- **Move Tracking**: Record complete movement history for analysis
- **Heat Map Generation**: Visual representation of attempted paths
- **Performance Metrics**: Completion time, total moves, optimal moves, efficiency score
- **Achievement Processing**: Unlock challenges based on performance conditions
- **Difficulty Adaptation**: Adjust maze complexity based on success patterns
- **Leaderboard Integration**: Track and display top performances

### 9. Wikipedia Maze Algorithm Implementation - Enhanced
- **Recursive Backtracking**: Implement proper algorithm to avoid simple paths
- **Hunt-and-Kill Method**: Create disconnected regions for complexity
- **Path Complexity Analysis**: Ensure routes have multiple turns and branches
- **Anti-Direct Path**: Prevent simple "L" shaped solutions to helmet
- **Winding Route Enforcement**: Force complex navigation patterns
- **Single-Width Maintenance**: Keep 1x1 paths while maximizing complexity
- **BFS Validation**: Measure actual path complexity, not just distance

### 8. Path Complexity Enforcement - Anti-Direct Routes
- **Turn Count Validation**: Ensure solution path has minimum number of turns
- **Direction Change Requirements**: Force multiple direction changes in solution
- **Winding Path Creation**: Avoid straight lines and simple geometric shapes
- **Complex Route Validation**: Measure path complexity beyond just length
- **Anti-L-Shape Logic**: Specifically prevent simple "L" shaped solutions
- **Maze Regeneration**: Regenerate if solution path is too direct or simple

### 9. Single-Width Path Enforcement with Complexity
- **1x1 Corridor Maintenance**: All paths exactly single-cell width
- **Complex Winding Routes**: Single-width paths that wind and turn frequently
- **No Wide Areas**: Prevent 2x2+ open spaces while maintaining complexity
- **Precision Navigation**: Challenging movement through complex narrow passages
- **Wikipedia Algorithm Compliance**: Use proper maze algorithms for complexity
- **Bottom Right Placement**: Helmet always at (size-2, size-2)
- **Wall-Free Guarantee**: No walls can ever appear at helmet position
- **Correct Route Guarantee**: At least one valid path from start to helmet
- **BFS Validation**: Use BFS to verify helmet accessibility
- **Predictable Target**: Players know destination, challenge is navigation

### 10. Fixed Helmet System with Complex Path Requirement
- **Bottom Right Placement**: Helmet always at (size-2, size-2)
- **Wall-Free Guarantee**: No walls can ever appear at helmet position
- **Complex Route Guarantee**: Solution path must be winding, not direct "L" shape
- **Anti-Simple Path**: Prevent straightforward geometric solutions
- **BFS Complexity Validation**: Ensure solution requires multiple turns and exploration
- **Predictable Target**: Players know destination, challenge is complex navigation
- Canvas text rendering for emojis
- Consistent sizing and positioning
- No external asset loading required
- Cross-platform emoji support

### 11. Emoji Rendering System
- Countdown from 30 seconds
- Starts on first movement
- Real-time display updates
- Game over on expiration

### 12. Timer Implementation
- **Waiting**: Show instructions, timer not started
- **Playing**: Timer active, accept input
- **Won**: Show victory with 👷🏻‍♂️ emoji
- **Lost**: Show "No Helmet" message with retry button

### 13. Game States
- **Retro Containers**: nes-container with-title for game layout
- **8-bit Buttons**: nes-btn is-success for retry functionality
- **Pixel Typography**: nes-text with color variants (is-warning, is-primary)
- **Authentic Font**: "Press Start 2P" for genuine retro experience
- **Responsive Design**: Container-based layout adapts to screen sizes

### 14. NES.css Integration Features
- **Clear Step**: Run "npm run clear" before editing to remove bundled JS files
- **Source Editing**: Focus on editing TypeScript source files (*.ts)
- **Clean Development**: Avoid conflicts between compiled and bundled files
- **Build Pipeline**: Clear → Edit TS → Compile → Bundle → Deploy

### 15. Development Workflow
- **Bundled JavaScript**: Single game.js file with all classes bundled
- **No ES6 Imports**: Avoid "Cannot use import statement outside a module" errors
- **Vanilla JavaScript**: Classes defined directly without import/export statements
- **Browser Compatibility**: Standard script tag loading without module type
- **Single File Bundle**: Timer, Labour, Maze, and Game classes in one file

### 16. ES6 Import Resolution
- Canvas 2D context for emoji rendering
- setInterval for timer updates
- Event listeners for WASD input
- Button click handler for retry
- Simple state management

### 17. Vanilla JavaScript Features
- 30x30 grid maze with single-width paths (1x1 corridors)
- Wall constraint: no walls at helmet position (size-2, size-2)
- Wikipedia maze algorithm implementation for complex winding paths
- Anti-direct path logic to prevent simple "L" shaped solutions
- Fixed helmet at bottom right corner with complex route requirement
- Extensive fake paths (35-50 dead ends) with BFS complexity validation (increased complexity)
- Advanced performance analytics with millisecond precision timing
- Achievement system with unlockable challenges and maze variants
- Adaptive difficulty adjustment based on player performance history
- Dynamic elements: trap cells, checkpoints, and optional moving walls
- Heat map generation for path analysis and learning insights
- Progressive scoring system with speed bonuses and efficiency metrics
- Emoji-based visual elements with NES.css retro styling
- 30-second timer countdown with precision timing and warning states
- WASD movement controls with automatic input blocking on game end
- Win/lose conditions with automatic UI display and performance analytics

## Build Process
1. Run "npm run clear" to remove bundled *.js files before editing
2. Edit single TypeScript file (game.ts) containing all classes
3. Compile TypeScript to JavaScript
4. Bundle into single game.js file without imports/exports
5. Serve index.html with direct script tag (no module type)
6. Remove unnecessary individual TypeScript files after consolidation
7. No external assets needed (emojis + NES.css only)

## Quality Assurance
- Every maze guarantees helmet accessibility at fixed bottom right
- Wall constraint ensures no walls appear at helmet position
- BFS complexity evaluation ensures optimal difficulty
- Single-width paths (1x1) provide precise navigation challenge
- 25-35 fake paths with measured complexity difference
- NES.css provides consistent retro aesthetic across browsers
- ES6 import issues resolved with bundled vanilla JavaScript
- Balanced difficulty: extremely challenging but always solvable within 30 seconds
